You need entries back in the order you inserted them, but you reached for a `HashMap`. The iteration order is undefined, so the output is unpredictable.

```java
import java.util.*;

Map<String, Integer> scores = new HashMap<>();
scores.put("alice", 90);
scores.put("bob", 85);
scores.put("carol", 95);

// Iteration order is NOT insertion order
scores.forEach((k, v) -> System.out.println(k + "=" + v));
// e.g. bob=85, carol=95, alice=90  (order not guaranteed)
```

Nothing crashes — the bug is silent. The data is right, but the order is wrong.

Pick the implementation that matches the requirement. `LinkedHashMap` for insertion order, `TreeMap` for sorted keys plus range queries.

```java
import java.util.*;

// Insertion order required -> LinkedHashMap
Map<String, Integer> ordered = new LinkedHashMap<>();
ordered.put("alice", 90);
ordered.put("bob", 85);
ordered.put("carol", 95);
ordered.forEach((k, v) -> System.out.println(k + "=" + v));
// alice=90, bob=85, carol=95  (exact insertion order)

// Sorted keys + range queries -> TreeMap
TreeMap<String, Integer> sorted = new TreeMap<>(ordered);
System.out.println(sorted.firstKey());        // alice (sorted)
System.out.println(sorted.headMap("carol"));  // {alice=90, bob=85}
```

Use `HashMap` only when iteration order is irrelevant; reach for `LinkedHashMap` or `TreeMap` the moment order or ranges matter.

Extend `LinkedHashMap`, pass `accessOrder=true` to the constructor, and override `removeEldestEntry` to evict once you exceed capacity.

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class LRUCache<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        super(capacity, 0.75f, true);  // accessOrder=true is the key line
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    @Override
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {
        return size() > capacity;  // evict when over capacity
    }
}

var cache = new LRUCache<String, String>(3);
cache.put("user:1", "Alice");
cache.put("user:2", "Bob");
cache.put("user:3", "Charlie");
cache.get("user:1");            // moves user:1 to the end (most recent)
cache.put("user:4", "Diana");  // evicts user:2 (least recent)
System.out.println(cache.containsKey("user:2"));  // false
```

That's the whole cache — three setup lines and a one-line override do all the eviction work.

Store events keyed by `LocalDateTime`, then use the navigation methods to find the next event, the previous event, and ranges of events.

```java
import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.TreeMap;

record Event(String name, String location) {}

var schedule = new TreeMap<LocalDateTime, Event>();
schedule.put(LocalDateTime.of(2024, 1, 15, 9, 0), new Event("Standup", "Room A"));
schedule.put(LocalDateTime.of(2024, 1, 15, 14, 0), new Event("Review", "Room B"));
schedule.put(LocalDateTime.of(2024, 1, 15, 16, 30), new Event("Planning", "Room C"));

var now = LocalDateTime.of(2024, 1, 15, 15, 0);
var nextEventTime = schedule.ceilingKey(now);  // 16:30 Planning
var lastEventTime = schedule.floorKey(now);    // 14:00 Review

var morning = schedule.headMap(LocalDateTime.of(2024, 1, 15, 12, 0));  // before noon
var workHours = schedule.subMap(
    LocalDateTime.of(2024, 1, 15, 9, 0),
    LocalDateTime.of(2024, 1, 15, 17, 0));  // 9am to 5pm range
```

Each call is `O(log n)` — finding the next event or a time window never scans the whole schedule.

Three everyday patterns: `merge` for frequency counting, `computeIfAbsent` for nested maps, and `getOrDefault` for safe reads.

```java
import java.util.*;

var orderCounts = new HashMap<String, Integer>();
var orders = List.of("ORDER-1", "ORDER-2", "ORDER-1", "ORDER-3", "ORDER-1");

for (var orderId : orders) {
    orderCounts.merge(orderId, 1, Integer::sum);  // frequency counting in one line
}
System.out.println(orderCounts);  // {ORDER-1=3, ORDER-2=1, ORDER-3=1}

var userPreferences = new HashMap<Long, Map<String, String>>();
userPreferences.computeIfAbsent(123L, id -> new HashMap<>())
               .put("theme", "dark");  // build the inner map only if needed

var config = Map.of("timeout", "30", "retries", "3");
var timeout = Integer.parseInt(config.getOrDefault("timeout", "60"));        // safe fallback
var maxConn = Integer.parseInt(config.getOrDefault("maxConnections", "10"));
```

Each line replaces three or four lines of null-checking and branching from the pre-Java-8 style.
